From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: 125hz <nickfugitives@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 29 Sep 2026 02:10:01 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] ntdll iOS: opt-in fastsync client for events and semaphores

The client half of fastsync (see include/wine/madeira_fastsync.h). A
handle is resolved to its cell once (get_inproc_sync_fd) and cached per
process id; NtSetEvent/NtResetEvent, NtReleaseSemaphore and
NtQuerySemaphore then work on the cell, and a single-object, non-alertable
wait takes a token with a CAS that carries the cell's generation, spins
briefly (an isb ladder, then an adaptive timed spin), parks on the cell for
at most MADEIRA_FASTSYNC_CAP_US (2 ms) and then falls through to the real
server_wait with the remaining timeout. A set or release that finds a
server-side waiter still sends a request, as a wake-only opcode (a release
of zero for semaphores), never a second set. A zero-timeout poll that the
cell proves is not signaled is answered locally, except one in 16. An
INFINITE single-object wait on a cell object waits in slices, and a slice
that ends with the cell signalled but the server saying otherwise demotes
that one object to the server path and logs once.

MADEIRA_FASTSYNC selects the mode: unset/0/off = off, "cells" = the poll
answer only, "auto" = the wake path arms after more than 20000
event/semaphore/wait operations in 10 s (MADEIRA_FS_AUTO_REQS), 1/on =
armed from the first call. MADEIRA_FASTSYNC_SEM=1 adds semaphores.
When this process was handed madsync's device the client stays off
whatever MADEIRA_FASTSYNC says: one in-process engine per session. The
policy is logged once, only when fastsync was asked for.

Default behaviour is unchanged: off, every hook is a couple of relaxed
loads and then the existing inproc_*/server path; the cache, clock reads
and log lines are never touched. close_inproc_sync() also drops a handle's
cache entry; madeira_fast_flush_pid() is for the iOS server.c
replacement's process init (paired Madeira change). No allocation or
logging in signal paths.

Validation: compiled for iOS arm64 with the flags of Madeira's
build/ntdll-unix/build.sh against upstream's configured headers; no new
warnings (the 6 existing -Wunguarded-availability ones are upstream's
futex helpers). The fork ran this client as its default across its device
logs.

Playport (decision 0054): carried in patches/wine-unix, taken from the
madeira-main reference branch's re-port (its wine-port 0076). sync.c is
compiled from this tree by Madeira's build/ntdll-unix/build.sh (no *_ios.c
replaces it), and patches/madeira-unix 0081 calls madeira_fast_flush_pid()
from server_ios.c. It applies after wine-unix 0006 and 0007 with a 3-way
merge in sync.c; the fastsync lines are madeira-main's. System APCs:
wine-unix 0002 keeps every system APC no waiting thread takes on its own
thread, never dropped. A fastsync wait not satisfied within its park (at
most MADEIRA_FASTSYNC_CAP_US, 2 ms) goes to the server wait, where the APC
runs; one satisfied in process leaves it for the thread's next server
wait. That is madeira-main's wine-unix 0002 rework; on this base it
needs no code, since Madeira's 074e0e3 drop is not in it.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Signed-off-by: 125hz <nickfugitives@gmail.com>

Madeira-commit: f6848ad4e98dab4f91b2a056d14112e16e65e958
Class: feature
Evidence: docs/evidence/2026-10-06-fastsync-on-8c050d0.md, madeira-main:docs/evidence/2026-10-06-portal2-madeira-wow64.md
